        {
          "secNumber": "1.4",
          "heading": "Task Parallelism",
          "content": "Task parallelism decomposes a computation by *activities* rather than by equal-sized portions of a dataset. A video pipeline may decode a frame, apply a filter, perform object detection, annotate the result and encode output. Those stages can overlap on different frames, and some stages may fork into parallel branches. A compiler can lex several independent modules while other workers optimize previously parsed modules. The important abstraction is a directed acyclic graph, or DAG, whose nodes represent tasks and whose edges represent dependencies. A scheduler can run any task whose predecessors are complete and whose resources are available.\n\nTwo lower bounds help evaluate a task graph. Let W be total work measured in time on one processor, ignoring parallel overhead, and S the length of the longest dependency chain, also called the span. On p identical processors, execution time cannot be less than max(W/p,S). If W=120 milliseconds and S=35 milliseconds, an eight-worker machine has a lower bound of 35 milliseconds, not 15 milliseconds, even before scheduling costs. Available parallelism is approximately W/S; here it is about 3.43. Adding processors beyond that ratio may improve schedule quality under some graphs, but the lower-bound analysis shows why ideal linear scaling is impossible. The critical path often changes when tasks have input-dependent durations.\n\nConsider a scientific data pipeline with independent modules for reading instrument A and instrument B. Calibration of A can overlap with reading B, but a cross-instrument reconciliation step needs both calibrated outputs. If calibration A takes 12 seconds, calibration B takes 18 seconds and reconciliation takes 4 seconds, the dependency-constrained portion takes at least 22 seconds. Making calibration A twice as fast cannot reduce that critical path unless it becomes the slow branch. This is a basic reason to optimize the critical path instead of focusing on whichever task has the largest CPU cost in isolation.\n\nTask granularity creates a tradeoff. A million tiny tasks of a few microseconds each can overwhelm a scheduler that pays several microseconds to allocate, enqueue, steal and retire each task. Coarsening tasks reduces coordination costs but may leave fewer runnable tasks when some finish early. Suppose 1,000 independent images require 2 milliseconds of work each. Giving 10 images to each task produces 100 tasks of roughly 20 milliseconds, which may be a useful balance on eight processors. Giving all 1,000 images to a single task creates no task-level parallelism. Giving each image 1,000 subpixel tasks introduces enormous scheduling overhead for little benefit. The useful granularity depends on variability and the cost of allocating and executing a task, which must be measured.\n\nWork stealing is a common response to unpredictable task duration. Each worker keeps a local deque of ready tasks and attempts to take work from another worker when its own queue becomes empty. Stealing can balance irregular recursive computations such as divide-and-conquer search, but it introduces coordination and changes execution order. Some schedulers favor locality by executing newly spawned subtasks close to their parent; some workloads benefit when large tasks are split earlier. Deterministic output requires explicit handling when tasks update shared results, regardless of how convenient the scheduler abstraction is.\n\nTask graphs also describe pipeline backpressure. If an expensive detection stage processes 40 frames per second but the decoder produces 100 frames per second, a queue before detection grows by approximately 60 frames every second unless arrivals are controlled, frames are dropped, or additional capacity is added. With an unbounded queue, memory usage can increase indefinitely. A bounded queue imposes backpressure on the producer, trading instantaneous throughput for system stability. Scheduling therefore includes resource constraints beyond CPU count: memory capacity, file handles, GPU streams and external service quotas can determine which tasks should run concurrently.\n\nFailure is particularly subtle in a task graph. If a task fails after writing a partial output, retrying it must not duplicate an externally visible side effect. For a pure numerical transformation, recomputation is usually safe; for a task that charges a customer or submits a job to another system, idempotency or transaction semantics are necessary. The graph should state whether completion means successful computation, durable output publication, or mere scheduling acknowledgment. A library's futures and promises help express dependencies but do not automatically implement exactly-once side effects.\n\nDesign a task-parallel solution by first drawing dependencies and resource requirements, then estimating W, S and scheduling overhead. Choose task sizes with sufficient work per launch. Measure runnable-task count and idle-worker time rather than assuming that an attractive graph necessarily executes well. Task parallelism complements data parallelism: a pipeline stage may itself contain a data-parallel kernel, and a task scheduler may launch many such kernels. The strongest design matches the computation's dependency structure instead of forcing every workload into one abstraction.",
          "simulations": []
        }
