        {
          "secNumber": "4.4",
          "heading": "MPI Concepts",
          "content": "MPI is a standard interface for message-passing programs. It describes concepts such as communicators, ranks, point-to-point messaging, collectives, user-defined datatypes, process groups and one-sided communication. It does not prescribe a universal network transport or require that processes execute on separate computers. A parallel program can run several ranks on one workstation and later distribute them across a cluster. The semantic contract belongs to the MPI version and implementation, while achievable performance depends on transport, placement, compiler and system configuration. Contemporary MPI standards include additional facilities for partitioned communication and other advanced patterns, but beginners should first master matching, completion and collective consistency.\n\nThe communicator is central. It defines a communication context and a group of processes whose ranks are meaningful within that context. Two libraries can duplicate or split a communicator to prevent unrelated messages from matching accidentally. Ranks are local identifiers; world rank three need not equal rank three in a subgroup. Splitting an application into teams for independent simulation ensembles is easier when each team uses a separate communicator. The program then has to decide how partial results are returned to a parent group, which introduces hierarchical communication design.\n\nA point-to-point message includes matching information such as source, destination, tag and communicator. A sender may use a derived datatype to describe strided or structured memory layouts instead of manually packing every field. This can improve clarity and sometimes performance, but type extents, alignment and portability must be checked carefully. A buffer of ten records should not be interpreted as ten bytes or vice versa. When workers use heterogeneous environments, portable representations and explicit type contracts matter more than assumptions about native structure layout.\n\nThe blocking/nonblocking distinction is only one axis of MPI semantics. Completion of a send may mean its original buffer is safe to reuse, while receive completion establishes that the receive buffer contains the matched message. Nonblocking operations return request handles; applications should complete or manage them according to the standard. Persistent communication requests can reduce repeated setup for fixed communication patterns. Collective calls place participation obligations on the group, and nonblocking collectives have analogous completion semantics. When trying overlap, instrument an actual timeline and test whether computation meaningfully proceeds during transfers.\n\nOne-sided communication, often called remote memory access, permits processes to expose memory windows and perform operations such as remote get, put or accumulate under synchronization rules. It can be useful for irregular access patterns but shifts complexity from explicit sends and receives to epochs, ordering and visibility. The absence of a matching receive call does not imply that synchronization disappears. Some one-sided patterns reduce overhead while others become harder to reason about than ordinary point-to-point communication. Choose the mechanism based on ownership, traffic patterns and the ability to establish correct memory visibility.\n\nParallel I/O is another important aspect. If a thousand ranks all open one file and write arbitrary overlapping regions, contention and corruption can arise. Collective file operations and well-defined offsets permit coordinated access. Storing one file per rank can simplify local writes but may overload metadata servers and create thousands of small files. Aggregated I/O combines data into fewer, larger transactions. Compression, checkpoint frequency, and storage system topology further affect the results. The computation's performance profile must include I/O when it is part of the user-visible workload.\n\nA minimal MPI correctness test should run at p=1 and p=2 before scaling further, use small recognizable data, and verify all communication buffers and counts. Test asymmetric message sizes where the algorithm permits them and force large payloads that exceed eager buffering thresholds. Use sanitizers where available and a watchdog timeout to detect hangs in an automated test. A benchmark that only exercises one implementation on one message size cannot validate all message-matching or progress assumptions.\n\nOfficial API documentation and standard revisions should govern exact function behavior. For teaching, it is more important to explain the lifecycle and ownership rules than to memorize call names in isolation: initialize the environment, identify communicators and ranks, establish data decomposition, perform messages or collectives, validate outputs, and finalize correctly. These principles remain useful even when MPI is wrapped by a higher-level scientific framework.",
          "simulations": []
        }
