          "secNumber": "5.4",
          "heading": "Divergence",
          "content": "Divergence occurs when threads that share an execution group follow different control-flow paths. In SIMT architectures, the hardware may execute paths under masks, so inactive lanes occupy execution resources without contributing useful work. The performance effect depends on path lengths, hardware reconvergence, compiler transformations and whether divergent groups are common. Divergence does not automatically make an algorithm incorrect, and avoiding every conditional statement is not always worthwhile. A uniform branch that every lane takes identically typically causes no lane-level divergence penalty in that group.\n\nConsider a GPU image kernel that performs a cheap transform on ordinary pixels but runs a costly outlier reconstruction on extreme pixels. If outliers are randomly distributed, many execution groups contain both ordinary and extreme pixels. Those groups may execute the costly path with only a few active lanes, wasting lane capacity. If outliers cluster spatially, groups aligned with the clusters may execute mostly uniform branches, reducing the effect. Sorting pixels by category could make execution more coherent, but the sorting pass and reordering memory accesses may cost more than the branch penalty. The optimal approach depends on how often the workload is repeated and whether categorized indices are reused.\n\nA common strategy is *compaction*: first generate a flag for items needing expensive work, then produce a compact list of their indices using a prefix sum, and launch a second kernel on only those items. The initial pass and scan impose extra memory traffic and launches. Compaction pays off when the expensive branch is sufficiently costly and sufficiently sparse. It can also enable separate kernels with different resource requirements, potentially improving occupancy. But it complicates output ordering and requires checking that the compact list is correct. A debugging test should compare the reconstructed output to a straightforward serial reference for every original index.\n\nDivergence also arises from early exits and variable-length loops. In graph traversal, one thread may scan a vertex with two neighbors while another scans a vertex with thousands. Lanes assigned to short lists finish quickly but may remain inactive while the long list is processed. Load-balancing strategies can assign high-degree vertices to cooperative groups and low-degree vertices to individual threads. That mapping requires classification and potentially additional synchronization. Optimizing only divergence without considering memory locality can hurt performance because adjacency lists may no longer be processed in cache-friendly order.\n\nBranch elimination is not always an optimization. Replacing a conditional with arithmetic selection might execute both sides of an expensive computation, increasing total work. It can also change numerical behavior, for example by evaluating a division that the original branch would have skipped when the denominator was zero. A predicate-based operation must preserve the domain and exception behavior of the original program. Measure whether the compiler already uses predication, and compare actual instruction counts and elapsed time rather than relying on source-code appearance.\n\nWarp-level primitives can accelerate operations such as reductions and shuffles, but their semantics depend on the set of participating lanes. Using them under divergent control flow requires that participation masks and synchronization assumptions are valid. If an algorithm assumes all lanes are active and some exit early, results can be undefined or incorrect. An apparently successful run with a full block may conceal the bug that appears only in the final partial block. Testing ragged sizes is therefore central to validating divergence-sensitive kernels.\n\nProfilers can report metrics such as eligible warps, branch efficiency, active lanes and instruction issue utilization. Each is a partial signal. High branch efficiency with terrible performance might mean memory accesses are scattered or the kernel performs unnecessary operations. Low branch efficiency might be acceptable if divergent branches are rare and arithmetic intensity is high. Evaluate overall throughput and isolate the dominant cause before changing code.\n\nDivergence management is ultimately a problem of matching heterogeneous work to execution resources. Keep similar-length work together when possible, use separate kernels for genuinely different computational paths when amortization supports it, and preserve correctness under all participation patterns. A consistent test matrix includes uniform inputs, mixed inputs, all-exception inputs, and a final incomplete group. This gives far stronger evidence than an attractive benchmark on one regular dataset.",
