          "secNumber": "6.2",
          "heading": "Scans",
          "content": "A scan, or prefix operation, produces intermediate accumulations rather than only the final reduction. For an array [2,3,5,7], an inclusive prefix sum returns [2,5,10,17]; an exclusive prefix sum returns [0,2,5,10] when zero is the identity. Both forms are valuable, but confusing them causes off-by-one errors. Scans support stream compaction, radix sorting, histogram offsets, memory allocation, sparse matrix construction and parallel recurrence transformations. Their central property is that every output is a reduction of a prefix of the input sequence.\n\nA sequential scan traverses the array once, maintaining a running accumulator, so it does O(n) work but has O(n) dependency depth. A naive parallel design might independently sum each prefix; that creates O(n squared) total work, wasting hardware despite abundant parallelism. Tree-based scans achieve much better work-depth tradeoffs. A common conceptual algorithm performs an upsweep to build partial sums, then a downsweep to distribute prefix values. For a power-of-two array, the two tree traversals have logarithmic depth and linear work. Each phase has dependencies that must be respected through barriers or kernel boundaries.\n\nThe upsweep-downsweep description is easiest to understand with eight items. Adjacent pairs first compute partial sums. Pairs of pairs combine, ultimately producing the total. The downsweep starts with the identity at the root and propagates prefix information: the left child inherits the parent's prefix, while the right child receives that prefix combined with the left child's aggregate. Leaves then contain exclusive prefix results. To obtain an inclusive result, combine each original input with its exclusive prefix. The logic preserves order for associative but noncommutative operators when traversal is arranged appropriately.\n\nOn GPUs, a scan is usually hierarchical because a single block cannot globally synchronize arbitrary other blocks in an ordinary launch. Each block computes local prefixes and a block total. A second scan computes offsets for the block totals. A final pass adds each block's offset to its local prefixes. This design incurs extra passes and temporary storage but scales to arrays much larger than one block. Advanced algorithms can fuse stages with carefully defined synchronization and progress guarantees, but these are harder to verify. A straightforward three-stage implementation is often the best teaching baseline.\n\nScan correctness requires an identity and a valid associative operator. For string concatenation, order matters: 'ab' followed by 'cd' must not be reordered as 'cdab'. For floating-point addition, regrouping changes rounding as in reductions, so mathematical associativity is only approximate in machine arithmetic. For integer counts, overflow behavior must be specified. If prefix counts exceed the maximum representable integer, the resulting memory offsets can become dangerous even when individual input values are valid. Use data types large enough for the total and check dimensions before allocating output arrays.\n\nStream compaction provides a worked design context. Suppose every input record has a predicate indicating whether it should survive filtering. Evaluate the predicate to obtain a zero-one flag for each record. An exclusive scan of flags yields the destination position of each surviving record. A thread with flag one writes its record to output at that position. The final output length is the total sum of flags. The procedure preserves input order and can run in parallel without output collisions because each surviving record has a unique prefix count. The proof uses the fact that prefix counts strictly increase at positions where the flag is one.\n\nScan performance involves more than theoretical depth. Each stage reads and writes arrays, and the algorithm may be bandwidth-bound. Temporary buffers, launch overhead and communication of block totals affect runtime. An optimized implementation may use warp-level primitives or fuse local scanning with later computation. Compare not only standalone scan throughput but also the surrounding pipeline: a filter-plus-scan-plus-scatter pipeline might benefit from processing tiles that stay in cache or shared memory. Saving one array pass can outweigh a marginal improvement in the scan's arithmetic operations.\n\nTest with arrays of length zero, one, prime numbers, and lengths slightly below and above block boundaries. Check both inclusive and exclusive conventions with a serial reference. Add input patterns of all zeros, all ones and alternating flags to validate compaction. A scan is a foundational parallel algorithm precisely because its output structure exposes ordering and synchronization mistakes so clearly.",
